it has been nine months since Lincoln  Park frontman Chester Bennington shocked  the music world when he took his own  life leaving behind his wife to Linda  and six children and now after grieving  privately his friend and former Linkin  Park collaborator Mike Shinoda is  sharing his grief publicly he's  releasing a new album called  post-traumatic about his journey from  the ultimate darkness to finally finding  the life listening to some of the songs  already they feel it feel really wrong  it started in a really dark place in the  beginning I wasn't leaving my house it  felt very claustrophobic  you know having lost my one of my  closest friends and collaborators and my  you know we associate like who we are  with to some degree like what we do  right and so I felt very lost and how  did you find that voice the experience  of this last year has been crazy and I  wanted to put it into music not only to  just like put my foot put like a stamp  on it and say this happened but also to  allow the fans and the and the people  who have been along for the ride to  guide them on that journey and say you  know in terms of keeping up with my  narrative or understanding where I'm at  [Music]  how difficult was the emotional process  of actually making the music and writing  those kind of writing those kinds of  songs well I mean that life was  difficult music was easy like the music  made things easier we started talking  about what about mental health like do  you want to get involved with for  example like suicide prevention you want  to get involved with organizations that  deal with depression and so on you know  even in addiction and I thought number  one some of those things I just don't  have very much experience with like  you'd think I do  because one of my best friends dealt  with them but I don't personally suffer  from those things and those  conversations I think that the most  powerful thing that I can do for people  is to actually take the things that I've  learned and share them with people so  for example with mental health I've  learned that mental health we treat it  like physical we should treat it like  physical health so if you say I'm  feeling unhealthy you could wake up you  say I'm feeling sick then what would you  do you'd stay home from work you might  take medication and if it's really bad  you might say oh I need to go see a  doctor this is really bad but we don't  for some reason we don't do that when it  comes to mental health is that maybe the  hardest part to deal with in this whole  thing I think like as human beings when  we see an ailment we're like oh are you  okay exactly yeah yeah yeah sometimes  you have to just feel sad and you have  to just wait until it kind of stops  happening right I had terrible days and  now I have less bad days and eventually  you know we'll file find a new normal  like a new stasis and that that is the  journey that I'm on that you know in  this album you'll see it happening as I  go out and start touring and talking to  fans and doing different conversations  and different people it'll evolve  [Music]  will you perform all the song speaking  about tour are the songs that maybe you  won't perform at first because you're  not ready to that's a great question I  actually thought about I had that on  that question in my head they become too  hard to play then I'll take them out but  I'm going to start by really going for  it and facing it head-on and seeing what  happens because what I realized at one  point in this whole thing is I was a  little bit apprehensive about meeting up  with large groups of fans because I knew  that a lot of them would be crying and  saying I'm sorry and ask me why what  happened and I was scared of that until  I did it and then I realized what a  cathartic experience it was for them and  myself
 

